/* Exam 3 "Hear the why": spoken explanations for q_L16.js and q_WHY_L16.js. Loaded after narration3.js. */
Object.assign(NARR_Q, {
"L16-001": "Activated charcoal is the absorption-prevention method that the patient-outcome data support best. Those data do not come from clinical trials. They come from patients who were treated and whose cases were written up. In class she said that, based on patient outcomes, charcoal is probably your best option, but not everything people ingest can be grabbed by charcoal. Charcoal also has its own risk, because a patient who vomits it can aspirate. Induced emesis is no longer recommended, its biggest risk is aspiration, and it is not first line. Gastric lavage and whole bowel irrigation are risky methods that an emergency team may still try in a severe overdose, but the outcome data do not favor them.",
"L16-002": "Single-dose charcoal works poorly four to six hours later because the poison has moved on out of the stomach and small intestine. Charcoal binds the poison only where the two meet in the gut. In class she said the poison was ingested orally and stuff moves on through, so the charcoal has to be in the stomach and small intestine while the poison is still there. She said the window used to be one hour and has been lengthened to maybe up to two hours. Her key gives the best time frame as less than sixty minutes after ingestion, with two to four hours as wrong. Charcoal is not absorbed into the blood; it leaves in the feces with what it bound. Enterohepatic recirculation is the reason for multiple-dose charcoal, not for the single-dose window. The patient's sedation does not set the window. The location of the poison does.",
"L16-003": "Inhibiting bioactivation means blocking the enzyme that would turn the poison into a more toxic metabolite, and it can be done only rarely. Bioactivation is metabolism to something more noxious than what was ingested. If the enzyme is blocked, the toxic metabolite is never made. In class she said there are not many cases where we can actually prevent the metabolism to a more toxic metabolite, and that methanol and ethylene glycol are the classic examples, treated with fomepizole or ethanol. Inducing detoxifying enzymes is not done in a poisoning, because induction takes days. Giving a cofactor such as sodium thiosulfate or N-acetylcysteine is enhancing metabolism. Pulling a toxin off its target, as pralidoxime does, is a pharmacological treatment.",
"L16-004": "Formic acid is the metabolite that causes the harm in methanol poisoning, and it damages the retina, so sight is lost. Alcohol dehydrogenase turns methanol into formaldehyde. Formaldehyde dehydrogenase then makes formic acid. In class she said that formic acid is the compound in the bioactivation of methanol that is toxic, that it kills the receptors in the back of the retina, and that you lose sight. Her key lists ocular toxicity and anion-gap metabolic acidosis for methanol. Formaldehyde is only the intermediate on the way. Oxalic acid and crystals in the small vessels of the kidney belong to ethylene glycol, not to methanol.",
"L16-006": "Ethylene glycol is dangerous because its end product, oxalic acid, can form crystals in small vessels and injure the kidney. Alcohol dehydrogenase turns ethylene glycol into glycoaldehyde, aldehyde dehydrogenase makes glycolic acid, and several more steps give oxalic acid. In class she said oxalic acid can precipitate in the blood as small microcrystals that block blood flow and damage organs such as the liver and kidneys. Her key says the active metabolites can crystallize in the microvasculature and that renal toxicity is common. The Marraffa article calls the toxicity primarily renal, from calcium oxalate crystals. Formic acid and loss of sight belong to methanol. Ethylene glycol is a substrate of alcohol dehydrogenase, not an inhibitor; inhibiting that enzyme is the treatment. Glycolic acid is only an intermediate, and stopping A T P production at cytochrome c oxidase is the cyanide mechanism.",
"L16-007": "Ethylene glycol is antifreeze, and cats and small children swallow it because it smells and tastes sweet. A leak under a car leaves antifreeze on the driveway. It does not burn going down, so a pet may lick it up and a small child may put it in the mouth. In class she said it smells and tastes sweet, so it is a problem for animals. The pink or blue color is a dye added to antifreeze; ethylene glycol itself is not a color. Windshield washer fluid and rubbing alcohol are not the exposure she described. Methanol exposures are different: most are in people with an alcohol use disorder who cannot get ethanol.",
"L16-008": "The antidote for both methanol and ethylene glycol is ethanol or fomepizole, and both work by inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase. The two alcohols share that first enzyme, so one antidote serves both. Ethanol competes with the poison for the enzyme. Fomepizole binds to the enzyme and inhibits it. Either way, methanol is not turned into formic acid, and ethylene glycol is not turned into oxalic acid. In class she said this is the way we block the metabolism and bioactivation of these two compounds to nasty metabolites. N-acetylcysteine is the acetaminophen antidote. Sodium thiosulfate supplies sulfur to rhodanese for cyanide. Sodium bicarbonate traps weak acids such as salicylates in the urine.",
"L16-009": "Ethanol works by competing for alcohol dehydrogenase, and fomepizole is used now because it is the stronger inhibitor with fewer adverse effects. In the old days an emergency room kept a bottle of Everclear, ethanol with a high percent by volume, and gave the patient a shot or two to outcompete the enzyme. Fomepizole is the prescription alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor used now, and in class she added that it gives better control of the dosing. Her key says fomepizole is a stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects than ethanol. Both block the same enzyme, so the toxic metabolites are never formed. Ethanol does not induce the enzyme, and fomepizole is not a cofactor.",
"L16-010": "The poisoning is suspected from the classic symptoms and confirmed by a blood draw that looks for the alcohols. These patients arrive unconscious, or awake but sedated. In class she said the key indicator is getting them into the emergency room for a blood draw that looks for these alcohols in the blood. More generally, the symptoms, often a toxidrome, give the first guess, and the hospital laboratory verifies the suspected compound if it can test for it. Urine pH belongs to ion trapping, not to diagnosis. A liver biopsy cannot identify an alcohol. Fomepizole is the antidote, not a test.",
"L16-011": "Acetaminophen, salicylates, iron and opiates are among the compounds most emergency rooms check for in the blood. They are common in overdose and can be measured in the blood. In class she listed acetaminophen, salicylates, iron, beta blockers and calcium channel blockers, then added opiates, and said there are a few others. The symptoms give the first guess, and the blood test verifies it. Cyanide does not belong on this list. It is a poison from the same lecture, the example of enhancing metabolism, but she did not name it among the common overdoses that emergency rooms screen for.",
"L16-012": "A toxidrome is a group of symptoms that, seen together, suggests exposure to a certain compound. In class she described it as a group of symptoms that makes you say this person has this, this and this, and wonder if they have been exposed to a particular compound. The toxidrome gives the first guess. A blood draw then confirms the suspected compound, if the hospital laboratory can test for it. The toxidrome table lists seven patterns: sympathomimetic, anticholinergic, cholinergic, opioid, sedative hypnotic, salicylate, and calcium channel blocker. Her key gives the opioid picture as somnolence, coma, slowed heart and breathing rates, and small pupils, so enlarged pupils are not part of it. A toxidrome describes the patient. It is not a blood test, not a list of antidotes, and not a class of poisons that share one mechanism and one antidote.",
"L16-014": "Inducing the detoxifying enzymes takes days, so in a poisoning a cofactor for the reaction is given instead. Enzyme induction is real, but the extra enzyme takes a few days to appear, far too slow for a poisoned patient. What can be done now is to supply more of what the reaction needs so it runs faster. In class she said it would be great to induce these enzymes, but it takes a few days, so the patient cannot be treated fast enough that way. Her two examples are cyanide, where sodium thiosulfate supplies sulfur to rhodanese, and acetaminophen, where N-acetylcysteine supplies ingredients for glutathione. Inducers are not avoided because they are toxic; time is the reason. Inhibiting bioactivation is a separate treatment.",
"L16-015": "Cyanide's target is cytochrome c oxidase, and A T P production stops. Cytochrome c oxidase is an enzyme of the electron transport chain. Cyanide displaces the oxygen within the enzyme, binds it, and blocks its work. In class she said that a person exposed to cyanide basically suffocates, because they are not doing any more A T P production. The patient is breathing, but the cells cannot make energy. Hemoglobin is not the target; it is used in the treatment, oxidized to methemoglobin. Displacing a toxin from the heme of hemoglobin is the carbon monoxide story, treated with high concentrations of oxygen. Acetylcholinesterase is the organophosphate target. Alcohol dehydrogenase bioactivates methanol on the way to formic acid.",
"L16-016": "Amyl nitrite oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, and methemoglobin pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase. Oxidizing changes the charge on the iron in hemoglobin. Hemoglobin has a form similar to the flat part of cytochrome c oxidase where cyanide binds. Methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so it is not good for the patient, but it is made on purpose. It takes the cyanide from the enzyme onto itself, forming cyanmethemoglobin and freeing the enzyme so respiration can continue. In class she said methemoglobin will not carry oxygen, but we still create it in our patients to save them from cyanide poisoning. Reducing methemoglobin back to hemoglobin is the job of methylene blue. Supplying sulfur to rhodanese is sodium thiosulfate. Amyl nitrite acts on hemoglobin; it does not bind cyanide in the plasma.",
"L16-017": "Sodium thiosulfate is the agent that enhances the metabolism of cyanide. Rhodanese is an enzyme the body already has. It moves cyanide onto sulfur from thiosulfate, forming thiocyanate, which is renally eliminated. The pathway has limited capacity, so sodium thiosulfate is given to donate sulfur and speed it up. In class she called this the part of the scheme that is really considered to be enhancing the metabolism and elimination. Amyl nitrite oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, which sets up removal of cyanide from cytochrome c oxidase. Methylene blue reduces methemoglobin back to hemoglobin. Hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide directly, which is inactivation, not enhanced metabolism.",
"L16-019": "N-acetylcysteine, called N A C, supplies ingredients the body uses to make more glutathione. Acetaminophen is metabolized to N A P Q I, a free radical that damages the liver. Glutathione, with the glutathione S-transferase enzymes, conjugates N A P Q I so it can be eliminated. The body makes glutathione all the time and keeps stores, but an overdose uses them up. In class she said N A C is given as a drug and gives ingredients to the system to create more glutathione. Her key says it replenishes glutathione. The idea that N A C neutralizes the free radical directly is wrong, and she said no to it. Blocking the enzyme that makes N A P Q I would be inhibiting bioactivation. Inducing enzymes is too slow for a poisoning.",
"L16-020": "Glutathione conjugates N A P Q I, with the help of the glutathione S-transferase enzymes, called G S T. N A P Q I is the free radical that acetaminophen is metabolized to, and it damages the liver. In class she asked what compound grabs N A P Q I and conjugates it, and answered glutathione, with the help of the G S T enzymes. Because an overdose can use up the body's glutathione, N-acetylcysteine is given to supply ingredients for more. Thiosulfate with rhodanese is the cyanide pair. N-acetylcysteine is what is given, not what conjugates, and alcohol dehydrogenase bioactivates the toxic alcohols. Methemoglobin belongs to the cyanide scheme.",
"L16-021": "N-acetylcysteine is usually given intravenously in the United States because it smells of sulfur, like rotten eggs, and is hard to swallow. It is a clear, oily liquid. In class she said it is pretty hard to get somebody to swallow something that smells like rotten eggs. The caution is that an oral product cannot be given into a vein, so where both forms are stocked, the intravenous formulation must be the one given intravenously, because of purity issues such as particulates. Oral N-acetylcysteine is absorbed and does work. Her key notes that it loses effectiveness about eight hours after an overdose but should still be given past that point; that is not why the route is chosen.",
"L16-022": "Cyanide and acetaminophen are the two examples of enhancing metabolism by giving a cofactor. For cyanide, sodium thiosulfate supplies sulfur so the body's own enzyme, rhodanese, converts cyanide to thiocyanate for renal elimination. For acetaminophen, N-acetylcysteine supplies ingredients for glutathione, which conjugates N A P Q I. In class she said her two examples are treating cyanide poisonings and treating acetaminophen poisonings. Methanol and ethylene glycol are the examples of inhibiting bioactivation, where the enzyme is blocked instead. Salicylates and phenobarbital are the ion-trapping examples. Organophosphates and warfarin are pharmacological examples, with pralidoxime and vitamin K.",
"L16-023": "The poison is reabsorbed from the forming urine back into the blood, and that keeps the poisoning going. Many compounds are filtered at the glomerulus into the urine being formed and then reabsorbed into the systemic circulation. In class she said you continue the poisoning by the reabsorption of that compound from the forming urine. Enhancing renal elimination means preventing that reabsorption, by ion trapping. The kidney is not making a toxic metabolite here. Return to the gut with the bile is enterohepatic recirculation, the reason for multiple-dose charcoal. Ion trapping is the treatment, not the reason the poisoning continues.",
"L16-024": "Intravenous sodium bicarbonate is given, and it makes the urine more basic. Salicylates and phenobarbital are mildly acidic. They are filtered into the urine, but at normal urine pH they are uncharged and get reabsorbed. Bicarbonate raises the urine pH, the weak acid takes on a negative charge, and the charged form is not reabsorbed. That is ion trapping, and it is very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital. In class she said the bicarbonate goes into the urine and charges that acid, and it cannot be reabsorbed when it is charged. Her key lists renal ion trapping to enhance elimination for salicylates. Acidifying the urine is the wrong direction for an acid. Cranberry juice acidifies too little and too slowly. Hemodialysis is an out-of-body method for very severe poisonings.",
"L16-025": "After bicarbonate raises the urine pH, a mildly acidic poison carries a negative charge, and the transporters that reabsorb solutes mostly move uncharged molecules. In class she asked what charge the trapped acidic poison will have, and answered negative. She said the transporters that reabsorb solutes from the urine back into the blood are active transport processes, and their substrates have to be, for the most part, uncharged. So the charged poison stays in the urine and leaves the body. An acid in basic urine loses a proton and becomes negative, not positive. Filtration at the glomerulus does not change; reabsorption does.",
"L16-026": "Ion trapping is not used for weakly basic poisons because acidifying the urine would mean acidifying the patient's blood, which is dangerous. Trapping works safely for slightly acidic poisons, by making the urine basic with intravenous sodium bicarbonate. A base would need acidic urine to become charged. In class she said cranberry juice acidifies urine only a little, not to a low enough pH and not fast enough to treat a poisoning. Weak bases are filtered and, while uncharged, they are reabsorbed, so trapping would help if it were safe. Bicarbonate makes urine basic, which leaves a weak base uncharged.",
"L16-029": "Inactivation applies once the poison is in the systemic circulation. Preventing absorption comes first. When that fails, an inactivator, what is officially called an antidote, can bind the poison in the blood so it cannot reach its target, and the complex is cleared. In class she said this works only once the compound has been absorbed into the systemic circulation. The two groups of inactivators are the heavy metal chelators, and the antivenoms and antibodies. While the poison is still in the gut, the absorption-prevention methods apply. Blocking bioactivation is a separate toxicokinetic treatment. Inactivation does not wait for dialysis to fail.",
"L16-030": "A good inactivator binds the toxin so it cannot reach its target, the complex is cleared from the body, the complex has no toxicity of its own, and the inactivator has high affinity for the toxin. In class she said the toxin and inactivator complex has to get cleared and must not have its own activity, and she changed low toxicity to no toxicity. High affinity matters so that massive doses of the inactivator, which could cause side effects, are not needed. A statement that the complex does not need to be cleared is false. The choice that does not belong describes binding the toxin's target tissue. Blocking the target is what a receptor antagonist does. An inactivator binds the toxin itself.",
"L16-031": "Heavy metal chelators are small molecules with a nucleophilic electron that binds the metal. They are usually multidentate, they bind the toxic metal more tightly than the body's macromolecules do, and they have low affinity for endogenous ions such as calcium. They bind metals such as lead, mercury, cadmium, iron and copper. A nucleophilic electron is an outer-shell electron that binds an atom with a more positive structure, forming a metal-ligand complex. Multidentate means one molecule binds one metal atom at several points. A chelator must not grab positive ions the body needs, such as calcium or the magnesium in some enzymes. In class she said a chelator must also be water soluble, non-toxic and readily cleared. Large proteins raised in an inoculated animal describe a polyclonal antivenom, not a chelator.",
"L16-032": "Multidentate means one chelator molecule binds one metal atom at several points. The figure shows a single molecule holding one copper atom through several binding sites. In class she said that this is what multidentate means: the molecule has several ways to bind one atom of the metal. It is not several metal atoms sharing one site. It is not one molecule holding several different metals at once. It does not mean giving several chelating drugs together. Each chelator does have its own list of metals, but that is a separate fact from how one molecule grips one atom.",
"L16-034": "Deferasirox is the iron chelator that can be given orally. There are two iron chelators, deferoxamine and deferasirox. In class she said deferasirox was nice when it came on the market because it is oral. It is orally bioavailable and was recently approved by the Food and Drug Administration. Deferoxamine is the older iron chelator, and her key says it chelates the circulating ferric iron. Dimercaprol, also called British anti-Lewisite, chelates gold, arsenic, lead and mercury, not iron. Edetate disodium chelates lead.",
"L16-035": "Wilson's disease is a copper problem, treated with penicillamine or trientine. It is a genetically based disease that produces very high copper levels, which damage organs. In class she said penicillamine, or now trientine, is used to chelate the copper out of these patients. Copper is not normally ingested in excess; Wilson's disease is why it is on the chelator table. Deferoxamine and deferasirox chelate iron. Edetate disodium chelates lead. Dimercaprol and succimer cover lead, mercury and arsenic, not copper.",
"L16-036": "A polyclonal antivenom is made by inoculating an animal with small, non-lethal amounts of venom and collecting the mixture of antibodies its immune system makes. The animal's blood is drawn, and the antibodies are filtered out. In class she said it is a collection of antibodies made to fight that venom, not one single genetically identical antibody. That is what polyclonal means. A single identical antibody from one cloned cell line is a monoclonal antibody. The source is an inoculated animal, not human bite survivors. A bitten patient has no time to make antibodies, so the animal's antibodies are given.",
"L16-037": "This is a type three hypersensitivity reaction, also called serum sickness, to the antivenom. The patient is already fighting the venom and is then given a flood of antibodies made by another animal. The immune system reacts to those foreign antibodies. In class she said people often have a type three hypersensitivity reaction, serum sickness, with fever, nausea and malaise, because the body is also fighting the polyclonal antibodies. That danger is why monoclonal antivenoms are being developed. It is not type one anaphylaxis. It is not unbound venom still acting. Aging belongs to organophosphates on acetylcholinesterase.",
"L16-038": "Digoxin immune Fab is an antibody product that binds digoxin so it can be cleared from the blood. It is made of sheep antibody fragments, called ovine Fab, raised to grab digoxin. Like any inactivator, it binds the toxin so the toxin cannot reach its target, and the complex is cleared. Her key says the fragments bind digoxin, which will be renally eliminated. In class she said it grabs digoxin and nicely clears it out of the blood. After it is given, total digoxin, free plus bound, rises, so serum levels cannot be used to calculate more doses. It is not a receptor antagonist at the heart. It is not a chelator, since chelators are small molecules that bind metals. It is not a cofactor.",
"L16-039": "The four categories are: the treatment is a receptor antagonist, the treatment is a receptor agonist, the treatment removes the poison from the active site, and the treatment bypasses an inhibited pathway. Naloxone, flumazenil and atropine are antagonists. Physostigmine, against the anticholinergic belladonna alkaloids, falls under agonist. Methemoglobin taking cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase, and pralidoxime taking an organophosphate off acetylcholinesterase, remove the poison from the active site. Vitamin K for warfarin bypasses the blocked step. Enhancing metabolism does not belong. It is one of the four toxicokinetic treatments, with preventing absorption, inhibiting bioactivation and enhancing elimination.",
"L16-040": "Naloxone is a receptor antagonist at the opioid receptor. In an overdose the opioid is the agonist, over-activating its receptor, and naloxone blocks that receptor. In class she said naloxone is basically an antagonist of an opioid receptor. Her key calls it a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors and warns that it can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users. Calling naloxone an agonist reverses the roles. Removal from an active site means pulling a poison off an enzyme or carrier, as pralidoxime and methemoglobin do. Bypassing an inhibited pathway is the vitamin K and warfarin category.",
"L16-041": "The benzodiazepine agonizes the GABA receptor, and flumazenil antagonizes it. In overdose the benzodiazepine causes too much action at that receptor. Flumazenil is the antidote and the antagonist. In class she briefly said the roles backwards, then corrected herself: the benzodiazepines are the overdose, flumazenil is the antidote, and the treatment is the antagonist. Her key calls flumazenil a competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor, and warns that it can cause withdrawal in a benzodiazepine dependent patient. Flumazenil is not a weaker agonist. Antagonism at the opioid receptor is naloxone; the benzodiazepine site is on the GABA receptor.",
"L16-042": "Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up, and atropine blocks the receptor the excess acetylcholine acts on. Acetylcholinesterase normally breaks acetylcholine down in the synaptic cleft. With the enzyme inhibited, acetylcholine accumulates and causes the cholinergic excess her key calls SLUDGE or DUMBELLS. In class she said atropine is going to antagonize acetylcholine. Her key calls atropine a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist. Freeing the enzyme is the other organophosphate treatment, pralidoxime. Acetylcholine is in excess, not depleted. Blocking acetylcholinesterase to raise acetylcholine is physostigmine, given for the anticholinergic belladonna alkaloids.",
"L16-043": "Physostigmine falls in the agonist category, because it blocks acetylcholinesterase and so raises acetylcholine. The belladonna alkaloids, with jimsonweed as the classic example, are anticholinergic: they block acetylcholine's action at its receptors. Physostigmine inhibits the enzyme that breaks acetylcholine down, so more acetylcholine is available at those receptors, and the drug acts like a receptor agonist. In class she said physostigmine is going to agonize those receptors. Blocking the muscarinic receptor is atropine, and it would add to the anticholinergic effect. Physostigmine does not pull the alkaloid off the receptor. The alkaloid blocks the receptor rather than depleting acetylcholine, so there is nothing to bypass; bypass is the vitamin K category.",
"L16-044": "The belladonna alkaloids cause dilated pupils, called mydriasis. Belladonna means beautiful lady, a name tied to the big dilated eyes these plants produce. In class she admitted mixing up the words for constricting and dilating, but she was clear that jimsonweed makes the eyes dilate. These plants are anticholinergic, and physostigmine is the treatment. Constricted pupils are part of the opioid toxidrome. Tearing is a cholinergic sign, the opposite picture. Damage to the retina and loss of sight come from formic acid in methanol poisoning.",
"L16-045": "Methemoglobin pulling cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase is the step that removes the toxin from its active site. Cyanide binds cytochrome c oxidase and stops A T P production. Amyl nitrite oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, which is made on purpose. Methemoglobin pulls the cyanide off the enzyme onto itself, so cellular respiration can continue. In class she said we had to create the methemoglobin to do that, but we are removing the cyanide from the active site. Amyl nitrite only sets up the removal. Rhodanese moving cyanide onto sulfur from thiosulfate is the enhance-metabolism step. Methylene blue reducing methemoglobin repairs the blood after the cyanide is already off the enzyme.",
"L16-046": "Pralidoxime pulls the organophosphate off acetylcholinesterase onto itself. The organophosphate bound to the enzyme inactivates it. Once pralidoxime takes the organophosphate, the enzyme's active site is restored, and the complex can be eliminated. In class she called pralidoxime a second way to treat organophosphate exposures. Her key says it removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase. It fails after aging, when part of the bound organophosphate has broken off. Blocking the muscarinic receptor is atropine. Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase to raise acetylcholine is physostigmine. Pralidoxime does not bind acetylcholine; the freed enzyme breaks it down.",
"L16-047": "The organophosphate has aged on acetylcholinesterase. Some organophosphates have a structure in which, after binding the enzyme, part of the phosphate group falls off. In the figure, a carbon, oxygen and hydrogen piece is lost, leaving a phosphate with a double-bonded oxygen, a hydroxyl and a methyl group. The aged organophosphate is then bound to the enzyme permanently. In class she said that once that happens, pralidoxime is not able to pull the organophosphate onto itself, and you cannot treat it with pralidoxime. So pralidoxime does not work in every organophosphate exposure. The route of exposure does not decide this. The order of atropine and pralidoxime is not the issue, and neither is the dose of pralidoxime. Aging is.",
"L16-048": "Aging is decided by the chemical structure of the organophosphate. Asked whether certain conditions make aging happen, she answered that it is the chemical structure of the organophosphate, whether or not that little bit breaks off, and then pralidoxime cannot grab it. Organophosphates without that structure do not age, and pralidoxime can still remove them. Atropine blocks the excess acetylcholine at the receptor, so it acts whether or not aging has occurred, and soldiers' kits carry both. The dose absorbed, the route of entry and the patient's genes do not decide aging.",
"L16-050": "Warfarin inhibits epoxide reductase, so active vitamin K is not regenerated. Vitamin K dependent carboxylase uses active vitamin K as a cofactor to carboxylate, and so activate, some coagulation factors. That reaction turns vitamin K into an inactive epoxide, and epoxide reductase regenerates the active form. In class she said warfarin inhibits the epoxide reductase, so less vitamin K is reformed and fewer coagulation factors are made. She also said it would be nice if warfarin acted on the carboxylase, because the mechanism would be easier to understand, but it does not. Warfarin does not break down coagulation factors; fewer are made.",
"L16-051": "Vitamin K is given for a warfarin overdose, and it bypasses the inhibited pathway by supplying what the blocked enzyme cannot regenerate. Vitamin K dependent carboxylase uses active vitamin K to activate some coagulation factors, turning vitamin K into an inactive epoxide. Epoxide reductase normally regenerates the active form. Warfarin inhibits epoxide reductase, so fewer active factors are made. In class she said we give vitamin K, kind of bypassing this blocked metabolic scheme. Vitamin K does not compete with warfarin at the enzyme. It does not bind warfarin in the plasma, which would be inactivation. Inducing new enzyme would be too slow; the product is replaced, not the enzyme.",
"L16-052": "Bruising, gastrointestinal bleeds and hemorrhagic stroke point to over-anticoagulation. Warfarin limits the regeneration of active vitamin K, so fewer active coagulation factors are made, and too much warfarin leaves the blood unable to clot well. In class she said the most obvious sign is bruising, that people can have gastrointestinal bleeds, and that the really bad outcome is a hemorrhagic stroke. The treatment is vitamin K. Dilated pupils, dry mouth and flushing are anticholinergic signs. Salivation, tearing and gastric cramping are cholinergic excess. Somnolence and slow breathing belong to the opioid toxidrome.",
"L16-054": "The guidelines were published in the journal Clinical Toxicology in two thousand five, and they are still followed, with no newer versions. In class she said they have not come up with new ways of treating these overdoses, so what was published in two thousand five is still what is followed. They would be rewritten only if something such as a new antidote came along. Staff at poison control centers, who answer the calls, have these documents memorized. They are journal papers, not part of the DiPiro textbook chapter.",
"L16-055": "The documents are evidence-based because the data, and the strength of each recommendation, are ranked. No one can be signed up to be poisoned, so the authors pulled published case reports and case series and evaluated them by the standards for evidence-based guidelines. In class she said they rank the data they have, and they rank the strength of the recommendations they make, and that is what is meant by evidence-based. They are not built from trials of overdosed volunteers. Evidence-based does not mean approved by the Food and Drug Administration. They have not been rewritten since two thousand five.",
"L16-056": "No vomiting at home; activated charcoal may be considered, but transport to the hospital is not delayed to give it. Her key for both drug classes says do not induce emesis, consider activated charcoal if there are no contraindications, and do not delay transport to the hospital to administer charcoal. In class she said charcoal is something done when the patient gets to the hospital, and that vomiting is not recommended anymore because it is too dangerous; the person might aspirate. Inducing vomiting is wrong for either formulation. The formulation sets the observation window, not whether to induce emesis. Giving charcoal at home before calling for transport is wrong, because transport comes first.",
"L16-057": "Found within six hours, the patient goes to the hospital; found after six hours with no symptoms, the patient can probably be managed at home. This is the rule for immediate-release calcium channel blockers and for immediate-release beta blockers other than sotalol. Within six hours, absorption may not be finished, so the patient could still respond badly. In class she said that by six hours all the drug should have been absorbed and they should be displaying symptoms, so a patient still without symptoms is going to be fine. Her key says to call the poison center, which will guide follow-up. She also said that if the amount taken is unknown, the patient probably needs the hospital. Home first and hospital later is backwards.",
"L16-058": "The window is eighteen hours for a modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil. Found within eighteen hours and asymptomatic, the patient goes to the hospital. Found after eighteen hours and still asymptomatic, the poison center is called, and the patient can probably be managed at home. The window is long because extended-release tablets can clump into a bezoar that breaks apart later and releases a large dose. In class she said that within eighteen hours you need to get them in. Six hours is the immediate-release window. Eight hours is the sustained-release beta blocker window. Twenty-four hours is modified-release verapamil.",
"L16-059": "The window for a sustained-release beta blocker is eight hours. Found within eight hours and asymptomatic, the patient goes to the hospital. Found after eight hours with no symptoms, the poison center is called, and the patient can probably be managed at home. In class she said the time frame for this one is within eight hours: you have to get them to the hospital. Like all extended-release products, these tablets can clump and release the drug late, so the window is longer than six hours. Eighteen hours belongs to modified-release calcium channel blockers other than verapamil. Twenty-four hours belongs to modified-release verapamil.",
"L16-060": "Modified-release verapamil has a twenty-four hour window. Found within twenty-four hours, even without symptoms, the patient goes to the hospital. Found after twenty-four hours and still asymptomatic, they are probably fine, and the poison center is still called. In class she said verapamil is really different. She also gave the reason: verapamil has multiple mechanisms and is not as clean a calcium channel blocker as the others, so the concern lasts longer. Six hours is the immediate-release window. Eight hours is the sustained-release beta blocker window. Eighteen hours is the window for the other modified-release calcium channel blockers.",
"L16-061": "Extended-release tablets can clump into a bezoar that breaks apart later. The chemistry of these formulations makes the tablets stick together in the stomach or small intestine. While the clump is intact the patient may have no symptoms. When it breaks apart, a large amount of drug is absorbed at once, which could be very dangerous. Her key says immediate-release products are absorbed more rapidly because they do not tend to form bezoars, while extended-release bezoars may break apart at unpredictable times, so no one can be sure when the drug will be absorbed. Potency per milligram is not the issue. These products are absorbed more slowly, not faster. Enterohepatic recirculation is the charcoal topic, not the window.",
"L16-062": "The windows differ because the case data differ, and the difference reflects the two classes' mechanisms of action. The authors pooled the overdose cases, analyzed them and set the windows from what the data showed. In class she said the slightly different time frames speak to the mechanism of action of calcium channel blockers versus beta blockers, that is, how each class acts on the heart. Both guidelines were published in two thousand five. Charcoal is considered for both classes. Clumping explains why extended-release windows are longer than immediate-release ones in both classes, not why the two classes differ from each other.",
"L16-063": "Call an ambulance. After a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker overdose, a patient can crash on the way to the hospital. The ambulance carries what is needed to treat that: intravenous fluids, pressors and paddles. In class she said that in a car stuck in traffic with a crashing patient, you are out of luck, with no intravenous fluids to hang, no pressors and no paddles. Her key gives the same answer for both classes. Speed is not the reason; equipment is. The formulation changes the observation window, not how the patient travels. Every patient who needs the hospital goes by ambulance, whatever the drug.",
"L16-064": "For calcium channel blockers, follow-up is left to the poison center's judgment. For beta blockers, follow-up calls are made at appropriate intervals for up to twelve to twenty-four hours, also at the poison center's judgment. In class she said the data behind each document showed slightly different details for the two kinds of cases. Swapping the two classes is the main trap. Six and eight hours are hospital windows for immediate-release products and sustained-release beta blockers, not follow-up schedules. Eighteen and twenty-four hours are the modified-release calcium channel blocker windows, also not follow-up schedules.",
"L16-065": "Refer the patient to the emergency department, then to mental health care. Her key gives the same answer for both drug classes. In class she said it is not just treating the poisoning: the patient is going to need help, counseling and evaluation, so that whatever is going on in their life can be addressed. A mental health referral alone, with the patient kept at home, skips the emergency department. Home observation with poison center follow-up is the plan for an asymptomatic patient found after the window, not for suspected self-harm. Poison center follow-up after the emergency department leaves out the mental health referral.",
"L16-101": "Multiple-dose charcoal is tried because the poison returns to the gut in the bile, where a later dose can bind it. In enterohepatic recirculation, a compound is absorbed, goes to the liver, is secreted into the bile and comes back into the small intestine. In class she said that if a compound does that recirculation and is grabbed by charcoal, you give the next dose, and maybe a third, while the poison is coming back into the small intestine, hoping to grab a little more. The bound poison leaves in the feces. The extra doses are about timing, not about the first dose running out of capacity. Charcoal does not work in the blood. Flushing the whole gut is whole bowel irrigation.",
"L16-102": "The team may try them because the overdose is severe enough that the patient may die. Every absorption-prevention method carries risk, and the outcome data favor charcoal. But charcoal does not grab everything, and it works only while the poison is still in the stomach and small intestine. In class she asked whether the team will try emesis, lavage or irrigation, and answered yes, why not: if someone has really overdosed and probably cannot be saved otherwise, these options may be worth their risks. The decision rests on severity, not on better evidence. The methods are not low risk; aspiration is the biggest risk of emesis. None of them is a required step before charcoal.",
"L16-103": "The guidelines are built from case data because no one can be enrolled in a trial to be poisoned. In class she said we really cannot run clinical trials and sign people up to volunteer to get poisoned. The outcome data come from people who were treated, whose cases or case series were then published. The guideline authors pulled those cases, evaluated them, and ranked the data and the strength of each recommendation. Case reports are not ranked above trials; they are used because trials cannot be done. Cost is not the reason, and the age of the documents is not the reason either.",
"L16-104": "Methanol gives an effect like ethanol, but it is metabolized to a compound that can cause blindness. In class she said that most methanol exposures are in people with an alcohol use disorder who cannot get ethanol and use methanol instead; they get the same kind of effect, except that they are metabolizing it to a nasty compound. Alcohol dehydrogenase and formaldehyde dehydrogenase turn methanol into formic acid, which damages the retina. Sweet taste and crystals in the small vessels of the kidney belong to ethylene glycol, the antifreeze exposure. Methanol is a substrate of alcohol dehydrogenase, not an inhibitor; blocking the enzyme is what the antidotes do.",
"L16-105": "With fomepizole given early, little formic acid forms, so the retina is spared. Fomepizole binds and inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, the first enzyme in the bioactivation of methanol. Without that step, formaldehyde and then formic acid are not made. Formic acid is the compound that kills the receptors at the back of the retina. In class she said that this is the way we block the metabolism and bioactivation of these compounds to nasty metabolites. The goal is to stop formation, not to speed it, which would be the cofactor approach. Fomepizole does not bind formic acid in the blood. Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate is a different kinetic treatment.",
"L16-106": "Identifying the poison is sometimes a guess because patients may not say what they took, and the laboratory may not be able to test for it. In class she said that people who intend to hurt themselves often do not tell you what they ingested, and other people are unconscious and cannot tell you. The first guess comes from the symptoms, often a toxidrome. A blood draw confirms it only if the hospital laboratory can test for the suspected compound. Toxidromes differ between classes of poison, which is what makes them useful. Emergency rooms routinely measure common overdoses such as acetaminophen and salicylates. The blood draw follows the symptoms; it does not come first.",
"L16-107": "Speeding up metabolism helps only if that metabolism does not create another toxic metabolite. In class she said the poison may be metabolized faster and cleared, if that metabolism does not create another toxic metabolite. Methanol and ethylene glycol are the opposite case: there metabolism makes the toxic product, so it is blocked instead. Because enzymes cannot be induced fast enough, the speed-up comes from cofactors, as with cyanide and acetaminophen. Being a weak acid is the condition for ion trapping. Being in the stomach and small intestine is the condition for charcoal. Induction takes days, not hours.",
"L16-108": "The treatment is not finished because the cyanide is still in the body, now bound to methemoglobin. Amyl nitrite made methemoglobin, and methemoglobin pulled the cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase, so the cell can make A T P again. In class she said we still have cyanide in the body; bound to methemoglobin it is not as damaging, but it has to be removed from the system entirely. Rhodanese, the body's own enzyme, then moves it onto sulfur from the sodium thiosulfate given, and the product is renally eliminated. The enzyme is not permanently inactivated; permanent binding is organophosphate aging. Methemoglobin does not release its cyanide into the urine.",
"L16-109": "Sodium thiosulfate speeds clearance because it donates sulfur to rhodanese, the enzyme. Rhodanese is an enzyme the body already has. It converts cyanide to thiocyanate, a less toxic product that is renally eliminated. The pathway has limited capacity, so on its own it clears cyanide slowly. Giving sodium thiosulfate supplies the sulfur group the enzyme needs, so more thiocyanate is made. In class she called this the cofactor step that helps clear the cyanide. Thiosulfate is not the enzyme; rhodanese is. Pulling cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase is the job of methemoglobin, made by amyl nitrite. Converting methemoglobin back to hemoglobin is the job of methylene blue, a reducing agent.",
"L16-110": "The patient still has a high level of methemoglobin, and methemoglobin carries no oxygen. The treatment made methemoglobin on purpose, to pull cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase. Once the cyanide has been cleared, the methemoglobin is still there. In class she said your blood cannot carry oxygen if the hemoglobin in red blood cells is in the form of methemoglobin. Methylene blue, a reducing agent, converts methemoglobin back to hemoglobin. The thiosulfate and cyanide product leaves in the urine; it does not build up or fall apart. Cytochrome c oxidase is no longer blocked, because the cyanide was already pulled off it.",
"L16-111": "N-acetylcysteine speeds clearance of N A P Q I because it supplies ingredients to make more glutathione. Glutathione, with the glutathione S-transferase enzymes, conjugates N A P Q I so it can be eliminated. The body makes glutathione all the time, but an overdose uses up the stores. In class she said N-acetylcysteine provides some of the ingredients needed to create more glutathione, so it works like a cofactor that enhances the metabolism and elimination of N A P Q I. She rejected the idea that it neutralizes the free radical directly. Blocking the enzyme that makes N A P Q I is not the acetaminophen approach. Inducing enzymes takes days.",
"L16-112": "Cranberry juice lowers urine pH too little and too slowly. To trap a weakly basic poison, the urine would have to be made acidic so the base becomes charged. In class she said you really do not get a lot of acidification of the urine from cranberry juice: not a low enough pH, and not fast enough, for someone who has ingested a basic poison. Anything strong enough would mean acidifying the patient's blood, which is dangerous, so bases are not ion-trapped. Cranberry juice does acidify urine, only weakly. Acidic poisons are trapped in basic urine made with sodium bicarbonate, the reverse direction.",
"L16-113": "An inactivator needs high affinity because a weak binder would have to be given in massive doses, and those doses could cause side effects of their own. An inactivator binds the toxin in the circulation so it cannot reach its target, and the complex is then cleared. In class she said a good inactivator needs really good affinity for the toxin, because you do not want to give massive amounts of an inactivator that on its own could cause side effects. Clearance of the complex is a separate requirement, and ion trapping has nothing to do with it. The inactivator binds the toxin, not the target. Inactivation acts after absorption into the circulation, not in the gut.",
"L16-114": "The part of the poison left unbound may still reach its target. An inactivator works by binding the toxin so it cannot interact with its target. In class she said that if an attempted inactivator binds only a little bit, the rest of the poison may still be active and find the target, so the inactivator has to grab it in such a way that the poison cannot touch the target. That is also why she changed low toxicity to no toxicity for the toxin and inactivator complex. Partial binding does not simply slow clearance. Bioactivation is unrelated. Binding calcium is the separate concern about chelator selectivity.",
"L16-115": "An ideal chelator has low affinity for calcium so that it does not bind up ions the body needs. A chelator must bind the toxic metal more tightly than the body's macromolecules do. If it also bound positive ions such as calcium, or the magnesium in some enzymes, it would strip them away. In class she said the ideal chelator has low binding affinities for other endogenous ions like calcium, because you do not want it to bind up the things you need. The concern is losing the body's calcium, not calcium making the chelator toxic, and not calcium displacing the metal. Multidentate is unrelated; it means one molecule binds one metal atom at several points.",
"L16-116": "The wait comes from cost: antivenom is too expensive for every small rural hospital to stock. In class she said that if someone is bitten in South Texas and is not near a big hospital, they have to wait for a hospital in San Antonio to get the antivenom to them. Hospitals know which others hold antivenom and how much, and there have been shortages, such as coral snake antivenom. The antibodies are raised in an animal ahead of time, not after the bite. The product comes from an inoculated animal, not from the patient's own blood. Serum sickness is a reaction that can follow polyclonal antivenom, not a test that delays it.",
"L16-117": "Warfarin lowers active coagulation factors because less active vitamin K is regenerated for the carboxylase. Vitamin K dependent carboxylase needs active vitamin K to activate some coagulation factors, and using it turns vitamin K into an inactive epoxide. Epoxide reductase reduces it back. In class she said warfarin inhibits the epoxide reductase, so less vitamin K is reformed, and without as much active vitamin K, fewer coagulation factors are made. It is an indirect mechanism. Warfarin does not compete at the carboxylase, does not break down circulating factors, and does not make the epoxide; the carboxylase reaction does.",
"L16-118": "A failing liver makes few coagulation factors, and warfarin cuts them further, so the patient bruises; vitamin K is given. In class she described working in a liver transplantation clinic, where patients had disturbed coagulation profiles because their livers were bad, and she said the work of creating coagulation factors is done by the liver. A patient still on warfarin was bruised on both arms, and the nurses gave her a vitamin K injection. Vitamin K bypasses the step warfarin blocks. Charcoal prevents absorption from the gut and does not restore clotting. More warfarin would worsen the bleeding. Deferoxamine chelates iron.",
"L16-119": "The patient can probably stay home because an immediate-release drug should be absorbed by six hours, so any effect would already show as symptoms. In class she said that past six hours all that drug should have been absorbed into their system and they should be displaying symptoms; with no symptoms after six hours, they are going to be fine. Found within six hours, the patient goes to the hospital, because absorption may not be finished. She also said that if no one can confirm how much was taken, the patient should probably go to the hospital. The point is absorption, not elimination. Clumping into a bezoar is the extended-release problem. Charcoal is a hospital step, not a home step.",
"TBL3-101": "Inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase blocks the formation of the metabolites that crystallize. Alcohol dehydrogenase turns ethylene glycol into glycoaldehyde, aldehyde dehydrogenase makes glycolic acid, and several more steps give oxalic acid. Oxalic acid can form small crystals in the small vessels and block blood flow. Her key says ethylene glycol active metabolites can crystallize in the microvasculature and renal toxicity is common. Ethanol competes for alcohol dehydrogenase and fomepizole inhibits it, so these metabolites are never made. Chelators bind metals, not crystals. Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate is the salicylate treatment. Supplying a cofactor would speed metabolism, the opposite of the goal here.",
"TBL3-102": "Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine depression because it competes at the benzodiazepine receptor. In overdose the benzodiazepine agonizes the GABA receptor, causing central nervous system depression with relatively normal vitals. Her key calls flumazenil a competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor. In class she said flumazenil is antagonizing the GABA receptor, which the benzodiazepines are agonizing, after briefly saying the roles backwards and correcting herself. Her key adds a caution: flumazenil can cause withdrawal in a benzodiazepine dependent patient. Flumazenil is not a second agonist. Binding the drug in the blood is the digoxin Fab approach. Competing at the opioid receptor is naloxone.",
"TBL3-103": "Naloxone competitively blocks opioid receptors, and that one action explains both the reversal and the withdrawal. The overdosed opioid is an agonist at the receptor, causing somnolence, coma, slowed heart and breathing rates, and small pupils. Naloxone blocks the receptor and reverses those effects. In a chronic opioid user, the same blockade can trigger withdrawal. Her key lists naloxone as a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors, with the concern that it can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users. Binding the drug for renal clearance is digoxin Fab, not naloxone. Naloxone is not a metabolic cofactor, and it is not an agonist.",
"TBL3-104": "Glucagon is listed because it raises cyclic A M P in the cardiac cells, which raises the heart rate. Beta blocker overdose causes hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status and respiratory depression. Her key says glucagon increases cardiac cellular cyclic A M P to increase heart rate, which counters the bradycardia. Her key lists atropine, calcium, glucagon, H I E T, which is high-dose insulin, vasopressors and intravenous fatty acid emulsion, and gives the same antidotes for calcium channel blocker overdose. Her key also gives glucagon's mechanism in calcium channel blocker overdose as increasing intracellular cyclic A M P. Inhibiting insulin release is octreotide, for sulfonylurea overdose. Driving calcium into the cardiac cells is calcium itself. Acting as a sink for a lipophilic drug is the intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion.",
"TBL3-105": "Atropine is listed against the bradycardia, and the vasopressors against the hypotension. Her key gives each mechanism: atropine increases heart rate, and vasopressors increase blood pressure. Beta blocker overdose presents with hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status and respiratory depression, and each listed antidote is aimed at a sign. Altered mental status is how her key separates beta blocker overdose from calcium channel blocker overdose, where the patient is often alert. Swapping the two drugs is the main trap. Neither drug is listed for mental status or for breathing. For organophosphate poisoning, her key lists atropine again, as a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist.",
"TBL3-106": "The emulsion helps only if the drug is lipophilic enough. Her key says intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough. Here a sink means a lipid phase that the drug moves into. A beta blocker that is not lipophilic enough will not move into it. Her key lists the same antidote, with the same mechanism, for calcium channel blocker overdose. Formulation sets the guideline time windows, not the emulsion's effect. A small volume of distribution is the condition for extracorporeal removal. Six hours is the immediate-release window in the guidelines.",
"TBL3-107": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments end the overdose by binding digoxin, and the bound drug is renally eliminated. This is inactivation: the inactivator binds the toxin, keeps it from reaching its target, and the complex is cleared. Digoxin immune Fab is made from sheep antibody fragments raised against digoxin. In class she said it grabs digoxin and nicely clears it out of the blood. Her key adds that after Fab is given, total digoxin, free and bound, rises, so serum levels cannot guide more doses. The fragments do not block digoxin at its site in the heart. They do not chelate potassium; raised potassium is a symptom, and chelators bind metals. They do not speed liver metabolism.",
"TBL3-108": "Sodium bicarbonate is given because it counteracts the block of cardiac sodium channels. Tricyclic antidepressant overdose brings tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures, sometimes vasodilation, an elongated Q R S interval and reduced cardiac contractility. Her key lists sodium bicarbonate for the elongated Q R S interval and says it counteracts the inhibition of cardiac sodium channels. For salicylates, her key gives the same drug a different mechanism, renal ion trapping, and that is the trap here. Raising blood pressure is the job of fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine. Binding the drug in the plasma is how digoxin Fab works.",
"TBL3-109": "Intravenous fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine are supportive care, to maintain blood pressure. Her key says these options are mostly supportive care, trying to maintain blood pressure. Tricyclic antidepressant overdose can bring vasodilation and reduced cardiac contractility, among other signs. The fluids and pressors do not act on the drug itself, and they are not ion trapping. Sodium bicarbonate is the one listed treatment aimed at the drug's effect on cardiac sodium channels, given for the elongated Q R S interval. Dilated pupils are a listed sign, but the fluids and pressors are not aimed at them.",
"TBL3-110": "Left at normal urine pH, salicylate would stay uncharged and be reabsorbed. Salicylates are mildly acidic. They are filtered at the glomerulus into the forming urine. Uncharged, they are reabsorbed back into the blood, and the poisoning continues. Intravenous sodium bicarbonate raises the urine pH, the salicylate takes a negative charge, and the reabsorbing transporters, which mostly handle uncharged molecules, do not take it back. Her key lists supportive care and intravenous sodium bicarbonate, with the mechanism renal ion trapping to enhance elimination. The negative charge appears only after the pH is raised. Crystals in small vessels belong to ethylene glycol. Filtration is the same either way.",
"TBL3-111": "Octreotide treats sulfonylurea or meglitinide overdose because it inhibits insulin release from the pancreas. Hypoglycemia is the only symptom her key lists for this overdose, so the antidote targets insulin, not the drug. With less insulin released, the hypoglycemia is countered. Stimulating insulin release would lower glucose further, which reverses the mechanism. Raising cardiac cyclic A M P is glucagon, an antidote for beta blocker overdose. Octreotide is not a receptor antagonist, and it does not bind the drug, which separates it from naloxone, flumazenil and digoxin Fab.",
"TBL3-112": "Deferoxamine is inactivation: it chelates circulating ferric iron. A heavy metal chelator is a small molecule that binds the metal with high affinity, more tightly than the body's own molecules do. The chelator and metal complex has no toxicity and is readily cleared. Her key says deferoxamine chelates the circulating ferric iron. Deferasirox is the other iron chelator, and it can be given orally. Deferoxamine is not a receptor antagonist. It does not prevent absorption from the gut; it acts on iron already in the circulation. Removing a toxin from an active site is the pralidoxime and methemoglobin category.",
"TBL3-113": "Two antidotes are listed because they act at two different points. Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up and causes the excess her key calls SLUDGE or DUMBELLS. Atropine is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist; it blocks the receptor that the excess acetylcholine acts on. Pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase, so the enzyme can break acetylcholine down again. In class she called pralidoxime a second way to treat organophosphate exposures. Pralidoxime fails if the organophosphate has aged on the enzyme. Swapping the two roles is the main trap. Pralidoxime does not block acetylcholine, and it is not a chelator; chelators bind metals."
});
